<section id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>The CMS manages the pages of the site, their content and everything around them. It all sits in the <strong>CMS</strong> section of the left-hand menu, where you only see the screens your profile allows.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Pages</strong>: the tree of the site, the content of each page, its publication.</li>
        <li><strong>Page templates</strong>: the models of a series of pages (a recipe, a case study, a news item), with the values each page of the series carries.</li>
        <li><strong>Tags</strong>: words that gather pages, such as "News".</li>
        <li><strong>Folders</strong>: the folders and contents of the shop, which are still managed apart.</li>
        <li><strong>Menus</strong>, <strong>Forms</strong>, <strong>Media</strong>: the navigation, the contact forms and the image library.</li>
        <li><strong>Components</strong> and <strong>Skills</strong>: the blocks pages are made of and the instructions the generator follows.</li>
        <li><strong>Settings</strong>: the mode of the site, maintenance, the error page, the generator.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>On every screen of the CMS, the <em>Help on this screen</em> link, at the top right, opens the part of this documentation that explains it.</p>
    <p>Every page exists in every language of the site. The language selector at the top of the screens picks the language you edit: title, address, content and publication are set language by language.</p>
</section>

<section id="pages">
    <h2>Pages</h2>
    <h3>Finding a page</h3>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Pages</strong> shows the tree of the site one level at a time. A page holding others shows how many and opens on its chevron. <em>Find a page</em> looks for a word in the titles of the language being edited; the publication state and the visibility narrow the search. The arrows that reorder pages only appear on the tree.</p>
    <h3>Creating and setting up a page</h3>
    <p>In each language, a page carries a title, an address, a summary, a detailed description and its search engine settings (title and description for search engines, sharing on social networks, canonical page, instructions to robots). For every language at once, it also has a parent page, a template, a tag, an image and a publication window (an optional date to go online and one to go offline).</p>
    <p>The <em>Image</em> tab picks the image of the page in the media library, with the picker of the sections (thumbnails, search, filters), or uploads a new one. That image is the one shown on the cards presenting the page elsewhere on the site, and when the page is shared on social networks.</p>
    <h3>The address of a page</h3>
    <p>The address is made of the addresses of the parent pages and the page's own one: a page "Advice" filed under "Our services" answers on <code>/our-services/advice</code>. Leave the <em>URL slug</em> field empty and it is taken from the title.</p>
    <p>Under the field, the screen shows the whole address the page will answer on in the language being edited. <em>Regenerate from the title</em> fills the field from the title: once a page has been saved, renaming it does not change its address by itself, and the screen points out when the address no longer follows the title. When another page, or the same page in another language, already answers on an address, a number is added to it (<code>cookies-2</code>) and the screen says so. Nothing changes until you save.</p>
    <p>Changing the address of a page, or moving it in the tree, keeps the former address as a redirection towards the new one: links already published and search engines follow. The pages under it follow too.</p>
    <p>The <em>Fixed address</em> field gives the page a whole address, kept as typed whatever its place in the tree, such as <code>about-us.html</code>. It keeps the addresses of a former site. The screen says, as you type, whether the address is free. Clear it to go back to the built address.</p>
    <p>A few addresses are reserved for the site itself (<code>admin</code>, <code>api</code>, <code>search</code>…) and refused.</p>
    <h3>Writing a summary or a description from a brief</h3>
    <p>Beside the <em>Summary</em> and the <em>Detailed description</em> of a page, and beside the summary, the description and the conclusion of a product, a category, a brand, a folder and a content, <em>Write from a brief</em> asks the generator for the text of that field. Say what it should say, and if you like its tone and its audience: the generator also reads the title, what the field already holds (to rewrite or complete it), and may search the products, categories and pages of the site to name them or link to them.</p>
    <p>The text comes back formatted (paragraphs, bold, italic, lists, links, subheadings) and is shown first, with what it cost. <em>Replace the text</em> puts it in place of the field, <em>Insert at the cursor</em> adds it where the cursor was, <em>Cancel</em> leaves the field as it was. Nothing is saved until you save the form, as if you had typed the text. The button takes the right to use the generator; its effort and length are set under <strong>CMS &gt; Settings</strong>, its model in the AI settings of Thelia.</p>
    <h3>Home page, duplicating, the bin</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><em>Set as home page</em>, on the list, serves the page on <code>/</code>. Its own address then redirects to the home page.</li>
        <li><em>Duplicate</em> makes a hidden copy of the page, with its tag and its content as a draft, to read over before publishing it.</li>
        <li>A deleted page goes to the <strong>Bin</strong> (<strong>CMS &gt; Pages &gt; Bin</strong>) with the pages under it. It stays there for 30 days, a duration the settings change, and can be restored until then. After that it is deleted for good, content and addresses included.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section id="content">
    <h2>The content of a page</h2>
    <p>Content is edited on its own screen, <em>Edit content</em>, opened from the page. It is made of <strong>sections</strong> laid one under the other, each one a component (a banner, a text, a list of products, a form…). On the right, the preview shows the draft as the site will display it.</p>
    <h3>Writing with the generator</h3>
    <p><em>Write it from a brief</em> sends the generator what the page should say, its tone and its audience. It answers with a whole page, which replaces the draft. To write it, it follows the skills of the site, picks among the components offered and may search the products, categories, pages and images of the site. If the settings allow it, it also reads the web pages the brief cites by their address.</p>
    <p>Then <em>Ask for a change</em> reworks the whole page, or a single section from its form, the others left as they are. The history of the screen lists every request with its outcome, its duration and its cost. The draft a request replaces is kept: <em>Previous drafts</em> lists the last five, and <em>Put it back</em> makes one the draft again, the current one kept in turn. Using the generator takes a right of its own, because every call is billed.</p>
    <h3>Editing by hand</h3>
    <p>Each section can also be edited by hand, in a form showing its fields: text, rows to add or remove for a list, a picker for an image or a product. A double click on free text in the preview edits it where it stands, with the toolbar of the editor; <em>Save</em> or Ctrl+Enter keeps the text, <em>Cancel</em> or Escape puts it back. Hovering a section in the preview shows <em>Edit</em>, which opens its form, and <em>Delete</em>, which removes it once confirmed. The form closes with its cross or the Escape key; another source for a picture than the media library, and the appearance of the section, are folded under their heading. The image of a section shows with its name and whether it is described; <em>Choose in the library</em> opens the library as thumbnails, with search, filters, tags and the sending of a new image. The width, the room and the alignment are chosen on buttons that draw them. An image of the media library is published with its alternative text from the library; the text of the section serves only a picture kept elsewhere, or an image not described yet. Choosing an image in a section takes the right to see the media. Sections are moved by dragging them or with the arrows, and removed. <em>Add a section</em>, under the last one, offers the components of the site: the one picked is added at the end of the page with an example text to replace. An empty page starts that way, without the generator.</p>
    <h3>Previewing and publishing</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Saving never changes what visitors see: you work on a draft.</li>
        <li>The preview shows the <em>whole page</em>, with the header, the footer and the title of the site, or the <em>content only</em>, the sections with nothing around them; the choice is kept from one page to the next.</li>
        <li><em>Preview the draft</em> gives a link valid for 72 hours, to send to someone without an account. A list sets the width of the preview to the ones the theme plans for (mobile, tablet, desktop).</li>
        <li><em>Publish</em> puts the draft online. An empty page, or one still holding the example text of a legal page supplied at installation, is refused. The structure of the headings is checked: depending on the settings, a problem is reported or stops the publication.</li>
        <li>Every publication keeps a version of the page.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each language has its own content: the French and the English versions of a page are two independent drafts, published separately.</p>
</section>

<section id="page-templates">
    <h2>Page templates</h2>
    <p>A page template is the model of a series of pages: recipes, case studies, news items. Each page has one, picked in its <em>General</em> tab. The templates of the site are under <strong>CMS &gt; Page templates</strong> (also reached from the settings). The <code>default</code> template always exists.</p>
    <h3>What a template sets</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The layout.</strong> The theme of the site may draw the pages of a template its own way. The templates screen says, for each one, which layout it gets.</li>
        <li><strong>A name and a description</strong> in each language.</li>
        <li><strong>A brief</strong>, with a tone and an audience, which the generator reads before the brief of each page of the template: it says what its pages are about.</li>
        <li><strong>Writing instructions</strong>, in markdown: how every page of the template is written (tone, length, sections expected, mandatory mentions). The generator reads them for each page of the template, like a skill kept to that template, and they win over a brief that says otherwise.</li>
        <li><strong>Fields</strong>: the values each page of the template carries, such as a cooking time or a city. A field is a whole or decimal number, a duration in minutes, a short or long text, a yes or no, a word picked from a list, or a date. It may have a unit, bounds, be written again for each language (texts only) or be required: a page cannot be published while it is empty. Values are filled in the <em>Fields</em> tab of the settings of the page, and the generator writes them along with the content.</li>
        <li><strong>A model page</strong>, the example content new pages of the template start from. It is never published nor online, and it is deleted with its template, never moved to the bin.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><em>Start a page from a template</em>, on the page list, copies the model page (its content in every language and the values of its fields) under the title you type.</p>
    <h3>Listing the pages of a template</h3>
    <p>The <em>Pages of a type</em> section, added to the content of a page, shows the pages online of a template: the latest news, every case study. It takes a heading, an order (most recent first, by title, by position in the tree, by a field) and a number of pages. The list is read on every visit: a page published tomorrow shows in it without publishing again the page holding it.</p>
    <p>A template some page uses, even in the bin, cannot be deleted.</p>
</section>

<section id="tags">
    <h2>Tags</h2>
    <p>A page carries one tag or none, picked in its <em>General</em> tab. Tags are created, renamed and deleted under <strong>CMS &gt; Tags</strong>. The theme uses them to gather pages, "the other news items" for instance. Deleting a tag takes it off the pages that carried it; the screen says how many.</p>
</section>

<section id="catalogue">
    <h2>The content of products, categories and contents</h2>
    <p>A product, a category, a content and a folder of the shop can also hold sections, language by language. Their screen in the back office has a <strong>CMS content</strong> tab, which says where their content stands in each language and opens its screen. That screen works like the one of a page: generator, editing by hand, preview, publication. The generator knows which item the content is shown on and what the item already shows, so it adds to the item rather than repeating its name or its price.</p>
    <p>The preview shows the draft two ways: in the <em>whole page</em> of the item, in place, or as the <em>content only</em>, without the header and the footer of the site.</p>
</section>

<section id="components">
    <h2>Components</h2>
    <p>The sections of a page come from a catalogue of components, of three families:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The components of the theme</strong>: the blocks the theme of the site opens to pages (banners, cards, lists of products, videos…). They render with the layout of the site.</li>
        <li><strong>Free text</strong>: a block of formatted text (headings, paragraphs, lists, links).</li>
        <li><strong>Live content</strong>: a block worked out on every visit, such as a form of the site or the list of the pages of a template.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>The Components screen</h3>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Components</strong> lists the catalogue: every component, where it comes from (the module, the theme, the site) and how many pages use it, drafts included. Switching a component off takes it out of what the generator may offer. Nothing else changes: the pages using it keep showing it and its sections stay editable by hand. Free text cannot be switched off. A button opens the toolkit of the theme, which shows every component offered, when the theme has one.</p>
    <h3>Having a component made</h3>
    <p><em>New component</em> describes to the generator what the component should show, what an editor fills in and how it should look. It proposes a version, which you keep, have changed or ask for again. A component kept joins the catalogue as "made on this site". Its texts are written by the pages using it, in every language. Making a component takes the settings right and the custom code right.</p>
</section>

<section id="skills">
    <h2>Skills</h2>
    <p>A skill is an instruction the generator reads before it writes: how a page is structured here, how text is written, what accessibility and search engines ask, how to present the catalogue, how to translate a page, how to stay within the design system of the theme. The module ships seven (page structure, copywriting, accessibility, search engines, catalogue, translation, design system); the theme may add more or replace them.</p>
    <p>Under <strong>CMS &gt; Skills</strong>, each skill can be rewritten, switched off and moved: the arrows of the list move it up or down one slot, and the generator reads the skills in that order. A skill <em>always read</em> is given to the generator with every request; a skill read <em>on request</em> is read only when the page calls for it, from its description. A rewritten skill keeps your text when the module is updated, and a button puts the shipped text back.</p>
    <p>An instruction you want followed on every page (a tone, a word to avoid, a mandatory mention) belongs in a skill. One that concerns a series of pages goes in the writing instructions of its template, and one for a single page in its own brief.</p>
</section>

<section id="menus-forms-media">
    <h2>Menus, forms, media</h2>
    <h3>Menus</h3>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Menus</strong> holds at least the main menu (<code>main</code>) and the footer one (<code>footer</code>). An entry points at a page, a content, a folder, a category or a product of the catalogue, a web address, or at nothing (it is then a group heading). Its label is optional: left empty, the title of the target is shown. An entry pointing at a category or a product follows its address in each language: renaming it or changing its address does not leave the menu behind. In the form, categories are shown as their tree and products grouped by their default category. The tree goes three levels deep; entries are moved with the buttons or by dragging them. An entry whose target is deleted, offline (a hidden category or product included) or not published in the language being read is left out of the menu in that language, and the screen says why.</p>
    <h3>Forms</h3>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Forms</strong> creates forms, placed in a page with the form section. A field is a line of text, an email address, several lines, a drop-down list, a tick box, one choice among several, a phone number, a date or an agreement to be contacted. Recipients are set here. Robots are kept out without a captcha. <em>Answers</em> are read, searched by email address, exported (CSV, JSON) and deleted; they go on their own after the retention period of the form (365 days by default).</p>
    <h3>Media</h3>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Media</strong> keeps the images of the pages (JPEG, PNG, WebP). The alternative text is required in every language, except for an image marked decorative. Each image shows its dimensions, its weight and the pages using it; an image in use cannot be deleted. The list shows 48 images a page; it narrows to the images still to describe in the language edited, the unused ones, the decorative ones or one tag, and is ordered newest or oldest first, by title or by weight. The search reads the title, the alternative text, the caption and the tags. “Describe them one by one” shows the images of the list still to describe, one at a time: Enter saves the alternative text and moves to the next one, “It is decorative” marks it decorative, “Skip” leaves it for later. Images are dropped on the upload frame of the list or chosen; each file is sent on its own, a refused file is named with why without stopping the others, then the describe queue opens on the images added. The sheet of an image says, for each language, whether it is described, to describe or decorative; its tags are suggested from those of the library, and a replacing file is shown before it is saved. Images ticked on the list take or lose a tag, are marked decorative or not, or are deleted when nothing uses them; those still used are kept and named. On publication, images are served in several sizes and in WebP.</p>
    <p>The images of the catalogue (products, categories, brands, folders, contents) are not in it on their own: the command <code>php Thelia thelia_cms:media:import-catalogue</code> copies them in, with their titles, alternative texts and tags. Run again, it copies only the new ones. A copy does not follow the catalogue: an image replaced there is copied again.</p>
</section>

<section id="settings">
    <h2>Settings</h2>
    <p><strong>CMS &gt; Settings</strong> gathers the settings of the site:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>What this site is</strong>: a shop, or a showcase site. As a showcase, the cart and the checkout are closed and the CMS moves to the top of the menu. Saving the showcase mode also creates an <em>Editor</em> profile (pages, menus, media, forms, none of the shop and none of these settings), to assign under <strong>Configuration &gt; Administrators</strong>; its permissions are yours to change afterwards. Going back to a shop reopens the cart and the checkout in one save.</li>
        <li><strong>Page shown when an address does not exist</strong>: a CMS page, served with the 404 status.</li>
        <li><strong>Days a deleted page waits in the bin</strong>: 30 by default, 0 never to empty the bin on its own.</li>
        <li><strong>Seconds a shared cache may keep a page</strong>: 0, the default, turns that cache off. Set it with whoever runs the hosting.</li>
        <li><strong>Close the site for maintenance</strong>, with the <strong>page shown during maintenance</strong> and the <strong>addresses that keep seeing the site</strong> (IP addresses). The site then answers that it is coming back soon, which protects its ranking. The back office and signed-in administrators are still served.</li>
        <li><strong>Generator</strong>: the <em>reasoning effort</em>, the <em>longest answer</em> and whether to <em>read the web pages a brief cites</em> (off until you turn it on: the server of the shop fetches the page and sends its text to the AI provider). A higher effort gives better pages, slower and more expensive, on models that read it. The texts written from a brief beside a field have an effort and a length of their own. The connection, the model and the budget are set in the AI settings of Thelia, not here.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Page templates, components and skills are set from their own screens, with the same right.</p>
</section>

<section id="permissions">
    <h2>Who may do what</h2>
    <p>Rights are given per profile, under <strong>Configuration &gt; Administrators</strong>:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>pages: the tree, the content, publication, tags, the content of the items of the shop, and this documentation;</li>
        <li>the generator: asking it to write or to change, a right of its own because every call is billed;</li>
        <li>menus; forms and their answers; media;</li>
        <li>settings: mode of the site, maintenance, error page, generator, page templates, components, skills;</li>
        <li>custom code: embedded frames (<code>iframe</code>) in published content, and making components.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A screen or a button your profile does not allow is not offered.</p>
</section>
